# net_drain: patches to the game's Client.py (generated from the dev's v0.5.84 text)

@@ # Hard wall-clock cap on how long a single _poll_network() call  @@
+    # Hard wall-clock cap on how long a single _poll_network() call may spend
+    # draining self._recv_q.  Without this, a backlog (server burst, or this
+    # thread simply not getting scheduled for a while) gets drained in one
+    # uninterruptible loop -- and since set_layout/_handle_line take
+    # self._lock repeatedly while processing each item, the render thread
+    # (which needs that same lock every frame to draw) stalls right along
+    # with it, which is what actually makes the WINDOW go "Not Responding",
+    # not just this thread. Confirmed live via /profiler: network_queue_wait
+    # hit a 10004ms max in a single frame -- a ~13,000-message backlog eaten
+    # in one shot. Capping the drain spreads that same backlog across many
+    # fast, responsive calls instead of one multi-second block; the queue is
+    # still strict FIFO, so nothing is lost or reordered, it just finishes
+    # catching up a bit later instead of freezing the client to catch up
+    # instantly.
+    _NETWORK_DRAIN_BUDGET_S = 0.008
+
     def _network_item_profiler(self):

